# Entry for `scrontab -e` on the cluster: every 15 minutes, a tiny job asks the web app whether a dataset is waiting and, only if one is, submits
# the real worker (cluster-worker.sbatch). Nothing in the repo reads this file; it is here to copy from. Replace <account>, <partition> and the
# paths (the lab folder from discussions/clusterSetup.txt, step 1). Needs the same setup as the hand-started worker (clusterSetup.txt, steps
# 1-6): the scripts, ~/.dv-worker.env, the token and the image. The Slurm options of the worker itself (memory, time) stay in cluster-worker.sbatch.
#
# How it behaves
#  - The check job is small (1 CPU, 200 MB, 2 minutes), so it starts quickly even on a busy queue. The big allocation is only requested when
#    GET /api/worker/waiting says something is waiting.
#  - No second worker is submitted while one is queued or running (MAX_WORKERS, default 1, counts jobs named dv-worker, whoever started them).
#  - A worker started this way gives its allocation back after WORKER_IDLE_SECONDS (default 300) without work, instead of the hour of a
#    hand-started one. Both settings can go in ~/.dv-worker.env.
#  - One log file, appended to (open-mode). Rotate it by hand. Try it first without submitting anything:  DRY_RUN=1 ./cluster-check.sh
#  - To pause: put a # in front of the schedule line. `scrontab -r` removes ALL your entries.

#SCRON --job-name=dv-check
#SCRON --account=<account>
#SCRON --partition=<partition>
#SCRON --cpus-per-task=1
#SCRON --mem=200M
#SCRON --time=00:02:00
#SCRON --chdir=<lab folder>/dv-worker
#SCRON --output=<lab folder>/dv-worker/dv-check.log
#SCRON --open-mode=append
*/15 * * * * <lab folder>/dv-worker/cluster-check.sh
